<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/dna-heredity">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    You began as a single cell carrying a library of instructions — and you now have roughly 30
    trillion cells, every one running on the same library, copied with staggering fidelity.
    The library is <strong>DNA</strong>: a two-stranded ladder twisted into a helix, whose
    elegance is its chemistry. The ladder's rungs come in four kinds — A, T, G, C — and
    <strong>A pairs only with T, G only with C</strong>. That one rule means each strand is a
    complete template for the other: unzip the helix and either side can rebuild its missing
    partner. Copying, repairing and reading the molecule all rest on this pairing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Scale check: your genome is about 3 billion base pairs — if printed as a book of single
    letters it would fill a thousand volumes — spread across 23 pairs of chromosomes and
    containing roughly 20,000 protein-coding genes. How the instructions got from a monk's
    garden to a programmable editor is a 150-year detective story; scrub through it below, then
    we will unpack each era's insight.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from monk's garden to programmable editor — 150 years in ten steps" widget="timeline" config={dnaTimeline} />

  <h2>Chromosomes, genes and the recipe book</h2>
  <p>
    The DNA in a human cell, stretched out, would run about 2 metres — so it is packed with
    spool-like proteins into 46 compact <strong>chromosomes</strong>, in 23 matched
    <strong>pairs</strong>: one chromosome of each pair from your mother, one from your father.
    This double set is called <em>diploid</em>. A <strong>gene</strong> is a section of DNA
    coding for one protein, sitting at a fixed address on a chromosome; the two chromosomes of a
    pair both carry that address, so you hold two copies of most genes. Alternative versions of
    the same gene — brown-eye vs blue-eye, say — are <strong>alleles</strong>. The four bases
    are an alphabet; three letters at a time spell one amino acid; chains of amino acids fold
    into proteins that do the cell's work. That is the whole reading scheme, and it is the
    same in bacteria, blue whales and bread wheat.
  </p>
  <p>
    Watson and Crick's 1953 paper was a turning point because the structure explained the
    function in one glance: antiparallel strands plus the pairing rule is a <em>copying
    mechanism</em>, which they announced in one of science's great understatements — "It has
    not escaped our notice that the specific pairing we have postulated immediately suggests a
    possible copying mechanism for the genetic material." The structural work itself leaned on
    Rosalind Franklin's X-ray diffraction — "Photo 51" measured the helix's diameter and the
    spacing of its turns; history has been correcting her credit ever since. And from 1966 the
    code was read outright: Nirenberg, Khorana and colleagues assigned all 64 DNA triplets to
    amino acids, finding (as the timeline notes) that nearly all life speaks this exact
    dialect — the strongest single clue that everything alive shares one ancestor.
  </p>

  <h2>Copying the library: mitosis, briefly</h2>
  <p>
    Growth and repair need new cells with the full, unchanged library — and that is
    <strong>mitosis</strong> (walked through phase by phase in the cell-structure lesson): the
    DNA is replicated, each daughter cell receives a complete diploid set, and the copy is
    near-perfect. The replication itself is <em>semi-conservative</em>: each new double helix
    keeps one old strand and builds one new one, so the original information is physically
    conserved — confirmed in a famous 1958 experiment by Meselson and Stahl. Errors do slip
    through (roughly one letter per few hundred million copied), and most are caught and
    repaired; the rare escapees are mutations, the raw material of both disease and evolution.
  </p>

  <h2>Halving the deck: meiosis</h2>
  <p>
    Sexual reproduction has a counting problem. Each child gets one chromosome of every pair
    from each parent; if parents handed over full diploid cells, every generation would double
    the count — 46 chromosomes becoming 92, then 184, and nonsense within a century. The
    solution is <strong>meiosis</strong>, the special two-stage cell division that makes
    gametes (sperm and eggs) carrying a <em>haploid</em> set: 23 single chromosomes, one from
    each pair. Fertilisation restores the full 46. Your gametes are therefore half-counts of
    you, shuffled — and here is the beautiful part: they are <em>all different</em>. In
    meiosis I, chromosome pairs line up and are dealt to the poles independently of their
    neighbours, so one human can make over 8 million (2²³) genetically different gametes by
    that shuffle alone. On top of that, matched chromosomes physically swap segments —
    <strong>crossing over</strong> — creating chromosomes that never existed before. Variation
    is not a bug of sex; it is the entire point.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> gametes are not "half a person" of each parent in any
  simple sense. Each egg or sperm carries a randomised mix of your mother's and father's
  versions — which is why full siblings (other than identical twins) share only about half
  their variable DNA, and why you are not 50% your mother and 50% your father gene by gene,
  but a fresh reshuffle every time.</p>

  <h2>Mendel's ratios, explained</h2>
  <p>
    Mendel, working with pea plants in the 1850s, crossed pure tall plants with pure short ones:
    every offspring was tall. Let those hybrids self-fertilise and the short trait reappeared —
    in a stubborn <strong>3:1</strong> ratio, across thousands of plants. His explanation, published in
    1866 and ignored for 34 years: each plant carries two copies of every gene; one allele can be
    <strong>dominant</strong> (visible whenever present) and one <strong>recessive</strong>
    (visible only in a double dose). The tall parent plants were TT; the short ones tt; the
    hybrids all Tt — tall, because T dominates. Self-crossing Tt × Tt, each parent passes T or
    t with equal chance, and the outcomes fan out into a grid (a Punnett square):
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="one cross, four outcomes — click each square to read its genotype" widget="diagram" config={punnettCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the grid: one TT, two Tt, one tt. Genotype ratio 1:2:1; <em>phenotype</em> ratio — what
    you actually see — 3 tall : 1 short, because the two heterozygotes look identical. The
    arithmetic is simple probability, and it works identically for humans: two carriers of the
    recessive allele for cystic fibrosis have, with each pregnancy, a 1-in-4 chance of an
    affected child. Inheritance of a single gene like this is <strong>monohybrid
    inheritance</strong>. Blood groups add a wrinkle: A and B are <em>codominant</em> (an AB
    person shows both) while O is recessive, so an A-parent × B-parent couple can produce
    children of every group. And most human traits — height, skin tone, intelligence — are
    polygenic, thousands of genes acting together, which is why they come in continua rather
    than neat 3:1 ratios. Mendel's peas were, in a sense, lucky choices of clean traits.
  </p>

  <h2>Mutations: when the copy changes</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>mutation</strong> is a change in the DNA sequence itself — a base substituted,
    deleted or inserted during copying, or a whole chromosome reshuffled. Mutations arise
    spontaneously (copying is near-perfect but not perfect) and are multiplied by
    <strong>mutagens</strong>: UV light (skin), ionising radiation, chemicals in tobacco
    smoke. Most are silent or harmful; occasionally one is useful, and that asymmetry —
    mostly noise, occasionally signal — is the raw material evolution edits. The sickle-cell
    mutation is the textbook case of the whole spectrum in one allele: one DNA letter
    swapped, one amino acid changed in haemoglobin, red blood cells warped into inflexible
    crescents. Two copies cause sickle-cell disease, serious and lifelong; one copy (the
    carrier state) does something remarkable — it protects against malaria, which is why the
    allele persists at high frequency precisely where malaria is endemic. A mutation's
    meaning depends on the environment it meets: that sentence is genetics and evolution
    and medicine in eleven words.
  </p>

  <h2>Sex-linked inheritance: a worked cross</h2>
  <p>
    Colour-blindness rides the X chromosome. A carrier mother (XᴺXᶜ — sighted) and a normal
    father (XᴺY) produce four equally likely outcomes: daughters XᴺXᴺ (sighted) or XᴺXᶜ
    (carrier), sons XᴺY (sighted) or XᶜY (colour-blind). Follow the logic: a son gets his
    only X from his mother, so <strong>each son has a 1-in-2 chance of colour-blindness</strong>,
    while each daughter has a 1-in-2 chance of carrying it silently. This asymmetry — no
    spare X to mask a recessive allele — is why the condition appears mostly in genetic
    males and why a colour-blind father cannot pass it to his son (the son got the Y). Haemophilia
    follows the same arithmetic; both were tracked through European royal pedigrees, in one
    of history's unintended genetic experiments.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pedigree charts — circles for females, squares for males, shaded for affected — let you
    reason backwards. Two unaffected parents with an affected child prove the condition is
    recessive (both parents must be carriers); an affected mother passing it to <em>all</em>
    her sons and none of her daughters points to X-linked recessive; affected father to
    affected son rules out X-linkage entirely. Reading a pedigree is the Punnett square run
    in reverse — and it is exactly how genetic counsellors estimate a real family's risk
    before a pregnancy.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> dominant does not mean "stronger" or "more common" — it
  means the allele shows when only one copy is present. Huntington's disease is dominant and
  rare; blue eyes are recessive and common in northern Europe. Dominance describes
  <em>expression</em>, not strength or frequency.</p>

  <h2>Genes are not destiny: the PKU lesson</h2>
  <p>
    One metabolic condition contains the whole nature–nurture debate in a test tube.
    <strong>Phenylketonuria (PKU)</strong> is a recessive disorder: children with two
    defective copies of one enzyme gene cannot break down the amino acid phenylalanine,
    which accumulates in the blood and damages the developing brain — and, untreated, used
    to produce severe intellectual disability. But since the 1960s a simple blood test at
    birth spots it, and a diet low in phenylalanine (strictly managed for life) produces
    completely normal development. The genotype is fixed at conception; the outcome was
    changed entirely by environment. No gene observed "in the wild" means anything without
    its surroundings — which is why modern genetics writes statements like "increases risk
    by X%" and why the honest summary of the whole field is: <em>genes load, environment
    aims, and the two are only separable in textbook diagrams</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    One pair of chromosomes deserves special mention. Twenty-two pairs are autosomes, matched
    twins; the 23rd is the sex-determining pair, XX in female-typical development and XY in
    male-typical — with the crucial asymmetry that mothers always contribute an X, fathers the
    coin-flip X or Y. The Y chromosome is small and carries remarkably little, but one gene on
    it (SRY) triggers the male developmental pathway. Because the X carries many ordinary genes,
    a recessive allele on it has no partner in XY individuals — which is why conditions like
    red-green colour-blindness and haemophilia show up far more often in genetic males: a
    single copy suffices.
  </p>
  <p>
    The library also contains far more than recipes. Only about 2% of your DNA codes for
    proteins; much of the rest was once dismissed as "junk", but decades of the ENCODE project
    and successors have revealed vast tracts of regulatory switches, RNA genes and structural
    elements — the instruction manual's wiring diagram, not its parts list. Meanwhile the same
    library supports the courtroom: DNA fingerprinting reads about 20 highly variable marker
    regions, and the odds of two unrelated people matching are astronomically small, which is
    how forensic genetics and the Innocence Project's exonerations work. CRISPR, the 2012
    discovery-turned-tool in the timeline, closes the loop: a bacterial immune system became
    programmable molecular scissors, and with it heredity turned from a subject of study into
    an object of editing — somatic gene therapies are already approved for sickle-cell
    disease. Whether we edit embryos, and on whose authority, is not a biology question; it is
    the ethics question this century inherited along with the code.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Mendel, <em>Versuche über Pflanzenhybriden</em> (1866); Watson & Crick, Nature (1953); Meselson & Stahl, PNAS (1958); Human Genome Project (Nature, 2003); Jinek et al., Science (2012).</p>
</TopicPage>
